[metadata][%
  title: Turn
  author: Scott Kovach
  date: [today]
%]

[slide][%Purpose%]
- Turn is for describing situations
- It weaves queries, action, and choice together.

[slide][%The now%]
A query describes a point in time, reading from top to bottom:
[code][%
turn T       -- during some Turn... [pause]
play-card C  -- a card is played... [pause]
action C     -- that is an action... [pause]
~activate C  -- so activate the card.
%]

[slide][%Kinds of Action%]
- ~episode: creates a new interval contained within now
- +fact: creates a new interval (left, Top), with left contained in now, extending indefinitely forward in time
- ^property: creates a new interval equal to now

[slide][%Match/Assert Symmetry%]
Matches and Assertions distinguished by prefix symbol:
[code][%
+action C     -- create an action card
~turn T       -- create a Turn (after `action` begins)
~play-card C  -- play the card (during that turn)
%][timeline]

[slide][%The now%]
Sometimes we need several related points in time, so we allow grouping, which temporarily shrinks the interval and pops the changes:
[code][%
-- "If you play the card you drew, ... [pause]
turn T
  ( draw-phase, draw-card C ) [pause]
  ( action-phase, play-card C )
  ...
%]
[pause]
The two phases do not overlap, but we can still match this situation and respond.

[slide][%Aggregates%]
- A tuple sees every tuple that fully contains it.
- We use this idea to implement aggregates: we can collapse a whole relation down to one value.
- Aggregates happen at some interval of time, and only aggregate tuples seen at that interval.

[slide][%Choices%]
- The purpose of a Turn program is to construct a choice set; it's a function from past decisions to future choices.
- The choice set is represented by a query; the query returns all the valid choices.

[slide][%Choices%]
[code][%
choose-card
  ?Card      -- introduces *choice variable*
  ~it Card   -- allows other queries to grab it
  !card Card -- asserts that any valid decision must be a `card`
%]

[slide][%Composable Choice%]
Other rules, besides the one that introduces a choice, can refine it:
[code][%
-- if "Earthquake" is active:
--   when choosing a card, it must be *sturdy* [pause]
earthquake, choose-card, it C
  !sturdy C
%]
[pause]
... that's it.
